===Verb===
{{en-verb|past2=smelt}}

# {{senseid|en|detect odor}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[sense]] a smell or smells.
#: {{synonyms|en|detect|sense}}
#: {{ux|en|I can '''smell''' fresh bread.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Smell''' the milk and tell me whether it's gone off.}}
# {{senseid|en|omit odor whether good or bad}} {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} ''Followed by'' '''{{l|en|like}}''' ''or'' '''{{l|en|of}}''' ''if descriptive'': to have a particular smell, whether good or bad.
#: {{synonyms|en|pong|q1=informal|reek|stink|whiff|q4=informal; these words refer to unpleasant smells}}
#: {{ux|en|The roses '''smell''' lovely.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her feet '''smell''' of cheese.}}
#: {{ux|en|The drunkard '''smelt''' like a brewery.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=8|passage=Philander went into the next room{{...}}and came back with a salt mackerel{{...}}. Next he put the mackerel in a fry-pan, and the shanty began to '''smell''' like a Banks boat just in from a v'yage.}}
# {{senseid|en|omit bad odor}} {{lb|en|intransitive|without a modifier}} To smell bad; to [[stink]].
#: {{ux|en|Ew, this stuff '''smells'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|smack of something whether literally or figuratively}} {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} To have a particular tincture or smack of any quality; to savour.
#: {{ux|en|A report '''smells''' of calumny.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Eikonoklastes|passage=Praises in an enemy are superfluous, or '''smell''' of craft.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2021|author=Taylor Swift|title=No Body, No Crime
|passage=Este's been losing sleep / Her husband's acting different and it '''smells''' like infidelity}}
# {{senseid|en|detect anything, odor or otherwise}} To detect or perceive; often with ''out''.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=II|scene=iii|passage=I '''smell''' a device.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To give heed to.
#* {{RQ:Latimer Lords Prayer|chapter=[The First Sermon]|folio=5|verso=1|passage=So from that tyme forwarde I began to '''ſmell''' the word of god, and forſoke the ſchole doctors and ſuch foolries.}}
# {{senseid|en|have the odor of}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To smell of; to have a smell of
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=IV|scene=i|passage=I do '''smell''' all horse-piss}}

====Usage notes====
* The sense “to smell bad, stink” is considered by some to be an incorrect (euphemistic) substitute for {{m|en|stink}}.

